Reason:
The first statement causes the method to return `null` when the end of the input is reached, without closing any open resources (the reader). This leads to resource leakage, as the reader remains open. The second statement in the `destroy` method also attempts to close the reader, but if `nextRow` has already returned `null`, this may never be called when multiple files are processed, resulting in the first file being processed without releasing resources.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (line == null) {
    closeResources();
    return null;
}
```
```java
public void closeResources() {
    if (reader != null) {
        IOUtils.closeQuietly(reader);
    }
    reader = null;
}
```
Reason:
The updated `nextRow` method now calls `closeResources()` when the end of input is detected. This ensures that the reader is properly closed, preventing resource leakage. The `closeResources()` method encapsulates the logic for safely closing the reader and setting it to `null`, ensuring that resources are released correctly when the input ends.

